  "summary": "Several new JPR studies benchmark DIA search-plus-quantitation combinations on spike-in mixtures and real samples, alongside glycoproteomics and DDA tool comparisons.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is evaluating DIA proteomics at the workflow level rather than ranking single search engines. One study crosses four search tools (DIA-NN, DIA-Umpire, diaTracer, MSFragger-DIA) with four quantitation utilities on two spike-in datasets. Another uses controlled human/yeast/bacterial mixtures on two MS platforms to benchmark DIA-NN postprocessing and derive quantification recommendations. A ZenoTOF plasma benchmark with DIA-NN and Spectronaut on spiked plasma found that DIA-NN library mode detected true changes best. A cardiovascular study compares three DDA and five DIA tools on real tissue and blood data, complementing the ground-truth designs. Beyond DIA, a glycoproteomics benchmark of five search tools on BJAB-K20 data adds diagnostic-ion post-validation of glycopeptide identifications. The Manchester Proteome Profiler, an open-source R Shiny app, covers downstream filtering, normalization, imputation, differential expression, clustering and enrichment. Together the items emphasize that processing and postprocessing choices, not just identification engines, shape quantitative outcomes.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
